id: pm-m0yjtg
title: "history-compact renumbers version identifiers, so pm get --at N and pm restore N address a different recorded state after compaction with exit 0 and no signal"
description: "Version numbers are the only addressing scheme pm offers for the immutable record besides timestamps, and history-compact silently rebases them. Before compaction pm get <id> --at 3 returned the state recorded at entry 3. After 'pm history-compact <id> --before 6' the same command returns the state recorded at the original entry 7, exits 0, and emits no warning, no deprecation, and no mapping. Timestamp addressing behaves correctly across the same boundary (a pruned timestamp is refused with history_target_out_of_range exit 2), so the two addressing schemes diverge: one is stable under compaction and the other silently changes meaning. Every stored reference to a version number - in a comment, a close reason, an external audit record, a script, a downstream tracker - becomes a reference to different content at the moment a stream is compacted, and nothing in the record says so. The synthetic baseline entry records only the free-text message 'history-compact baseline snapshot before 6.' and carries no structured field for the compacted-entry count or the version offset, so no consumer can mechanically translate a pre-compaction version to its post-compaction equivalent or determine that a given version number predates the boundary. The baseline entry is also stamped with the compaction wall-clock time while occupying index 1, so after compaction the stream's index order and timestamp order disagree: index 1 ts 14:55:39 precedes index 2 ts 14:54:53. pm history --verify --strict-exit exits 0 throughout, so no integrity check observes any of this."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: agent-ux,"area:history","area:storage",durability,immutability
created_at: "2026-08-02T15:01:30.323Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-04T21:05:46.600Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-04T21:05:46.002Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-04T21:05:46.002Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 600
acceptance_criteria: "A version identifier resolves to the same recorded state before and after compaction, or the pre-boundary identifier is refused with a distinct machine-readable code rather than silently resolving to different content; The compaction baseline entry records the compacted-entry count and the version offset as structured fields, so a reader can mechanically map a pre-compaction version number to its post-compaction equivalent or classify it as pruned; A read or restore targeting a version that predates the compaction boundary is reported as intentionally unavailable, distinguishable by code from an out-of-range target that never existed; The stream's index order and timestamp order agree after compaction, or the baseline entry's timestamp semantics are declared in the format specification and asserted by a test; history --verify fails, or a dedicated check fails, on a stream whose version addressing changed meaning without a recorded offset - proven by a negative-control fixture; The evidence in this item's description is reproduced as a regression test that compacts a stream and asserts version-identifier stability or refusal"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Make version addressing stable under compaction, or make its instability explicit, recorded, and refusable"
value: Restoring an item to any version at any time is the core durability promise; an identifier that silently changes meaning turns a correct-looking restore into undetected data substitution
impact: Silent wrong answer on point-in-time reads and restores after any compaction
why_now: "The mandate is that any item can be restored to any version at any time and that the record is immutable proof; compaction is the one shipped operation that breaks the identifier the promise is written in, and it passes every existing integrity check"
parent: pm-o2kc
risk: high
confidence: high
severity: high
environment: "pm 2026.8.2, sandbox PM_PATH, Node 20+, linux"
repro_steps: pm create Task V1; for i in 2..10 pm update <id> --title V$i; pm get <id> --at 3 -> V3; pm history-compact <id> --before 6; pm get <id> --at 3 -> V7 (exit 0)
resolution: "Preserve durable version addresses, original retained records and historical hash surfaces through repeated compaction; publish structured offsets and nullable legacy mapping contracts."
expected_result: Retained numeric addresses resolve to the same state; pruned and unknowable legacy versions receive distinct typed refusals; timestamp order and checkpoint semantics remain explicit.
actual_result: "Repeated-compaction, legacy epoch 1 and early epoch 2 provenance fixtures passed. Retained versions matched independently captured states; unknown legacy timestamp reads and restoration report null version addresses."
