comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-02T15:01:30.323Z","harness:claude-code","Duplicate check before create: searched 'immutable history restore version', 'compaction', 'point in time', and read pm-wlqxg3, pm-h6wmtc, pm-3z0k, pm-9yfo, pm-6l2mza, pm-hib1, pm-4a7m in full. pm-wlqxg3 is the reconstruction GATE and its AC already anticipates this class ('versions it dropped are reported as intentionally unavailable rather than silently wrong') but it is the test that would catch the defect, not the product behaviour - filed separately per the standing gate-vs-product-defect split (precedent pm-0fhw). pm-h6wmtc owns retention POLICY and lawful erasure, not identifier stability. pm-6l2mza (workspace snapshot rewind) is CLOSED and concerns deleting streams, not renumbering within one. pm-3z0k is detached attestation export. No existing item owns version-identifier stability across compaction."
  "2026-08-07T17:13:33.159Z","harness:claude-code","Reproduced live 2026-08-07 in an isolated sandbox, and the reproduction shows the two addressing schemes have opposite safety properties on the same verb.\n\nNine history entries were built on one item, each update setting a distinguishable description. Before compaction, `pm restore <id> 3` exits 0 and yields description \"version 2\", which is the state entry 3 recorded. `pm history-compact <id> --before 6` then reports entries_compacted 5, entries_retained 5, first_retained_entry 6, and rewrites the stream as a synthetic baseline plus the retained tail, renumbered densely from 1. The identical call `pm restore <id> 3` now exits 0 and yields \"version 6\". Same command, same item, same repository, different reconstructed state, no warning and no offset in the envelope.\n\nTwo additional facts that sharpen the AC set rather than widening it. First, `pm restore <id> 1` succeeds and yields \"version 4\": version 1 is now the history_compact_baseline, whose replay reconstructs the state at the compaction boundary, so the earliest addressable version is not the earliest recorded version and nothing says so. Second, the timestamp scheme is correct where the version scheme is not: a pre-boundary ISO timestamp is refused with history_target_out_of_range at exit 2, and an out-of-range high version number (99) is also refused with the same code. So the refusal machinery already exists and is reached from both ends of the range; it cannot fire in the middle because after compaction the numbering is dense from 1 again and a pruned version is indistinguishable from a present one.\n\nThroughout all of it `pm history <id> --verify --strict-exit` exits 0. The record is provably intact and provably mis-addressed at the same time, which is the state AC3 exists to make impossible. The point-in-time read has the same defect as the mutation: `pm get <id> --at 3` returns the post-compaction state with no signal. The mutation is the worse half, because a restore appends a hash-anchored entry whose recorded intent no longer denotes what it denoted when it was written."
  "2026-09-04T19:57:31.334Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: shared durable-version resolver preserves retained addresses through repeated compaction, records checkpoint offsets and last-compacted timestamps, refuses unmapped legacy numeric addresses, and reports physical versus durable coordinates separately. Historical claim-state projection now slices using the resolved physical index. Focused compaction suite passes 45 tests; broader coverage is running."
  "2026-09-04T20:24:25.111Z","harness:codex","TDD extension: two new negative controls reproduce compaction failure on otherwise verified field-frozen epoch-1 and early epoch-2 streams containing linked-test provenance. Both fail at the compactor current-epoch after-hash check. The durable-address implementation must preserve retained records and respect their recorded hash variants rather than rehashing legacy metadata with the current algorithm."
  "2026-09-04T20:26:41.286Z","harness:codex","Implementation: compaction keeps retained records verbatim, preserves the checkpoint hash variant and explicit/unversioned epoch marker, and uses the central epoch-aware verifier. Removed redundant current-epoch hash checks and the two mock-only tests that forced them past a successful precheck. Replacement real-stream regressions cover linked-test provenance in epoch 1 and early epoch 2, repeated compaction, stable version addresses and independently captured states."
  "2026-09-04T21:05:45.317Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: Preserve durable version addresses, original retained records and historical hash surfaces through repeated compaction; publish structured offsets and nullable legacy mapping contracts. Repeated-compaction, legacy epoch 1 and early epoch 2 provenance fixtures passed. Retained versions matched independently captured states; unknown legacy timestamp reads and restoration report null version addresses. Repository-wide exact 100/100/100/100 coverage and pnpm quality:static passed without weakening thresholds. All changed surfaces are linked to canonical delivery items. Hosted PR review and merged-main operational verification are pending and must pass before merge; the already published 2026.9.4 artifact does not contain this branch."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-04T20:42:34.026Z","harness:codex",Compaction must preserve both durable addressing and historical hash surfaces. Valid field-frozen epoch-1/early-epoch-2 records can fail if redundantly checked with the current hash algorithm; retain record contents and seal the checkpoint using its verified historical endpoints.
